WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.719
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.719 --> 00:00:06.269
<v Tutor>It's Saturday the thirty-first of October, and this is the Saturday spiral review.

3
00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:14.965
<v Tutor>Spiral means we come back to things you've already met, but harder, and joined to another system, because that is exactly how the College writes its long questions.

4
00:00:14.965 --> 00:00:16.937
<v Tutor>Today there are three revisits.

5
00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:24.470
<v Tutor>First, shock and fluids, from this week: how you prove fluid responsiveness when the usual tests fail, and the rise and fall of the starches.

6
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.172
<v Tutor>Second, from three weeks ago, community-acquired pneumonia that isn't getting better.

7
00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:34.531
<v Tutor>Third, also from three weeks ago, lactate after cardiac surgery, with a blood gas to work through.

8
00:00:34.531 --> 00:00:39.384
<v Tutor>Then a journal watch on PELICAN, a new randomised trial of cardiac index guided therapy.

9
00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:49.344
<v Tutor>And we finish with a syllabus round-up: blood gas analysis, interpreting arterial gases, diabetes insipidus, the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone, and electrolyte replacement.

10
00:00:49.344 --> 00:00:51.720
<v Tutor>There are six pause and answer moments.

11
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.400
<v Tutor>When you hear them, stop and answer out loud.

12
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.885
<v Tutor>Revisit one.

13
00:00:56.885 --> 00:00:58.242
<v Tutor>Shock and fluids.

14
00:00:58.242 --> 00:00:59.381
<v Tutor>Picture this.

15
00:00:59.381 --> 00:01:00.900
<v Tutor>Day two in the unit.

16
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.078
<v Tutor>A sixty-year-old man with faecal peritonitis, back from a laparotomy.

17
00:01:05.078 --> 00:01:08.117
<v Tutor>He's ventilated at six millilitres per kilogram.

18
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:11.372
<v Tutor>He's in atrial fibrillation at a hundred and thirty.

19
00:01:11.372 --> 00:01:15.767
<v Tutor>Noradrenaline is at 0.25 micrograms per kilogram per minute and climbing.

20
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:21.247
<v Tutor>Urine output has been ten millilitres an hour for four hours, and his creatinine has doubled.

21
00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:26.022
<v Tutor>He's had six litres of crystalloid since he arrived in the emergency department.

22
00:01:26.022 --> 00:01:28.464
<v Tutor>The nurse asks you a simple question.

23
00:01:28.464 --> 00:01:30.200
<v Tutor>Does he need more fluid?

24
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:37.362
<v Tutor>That question has been asked by the College three times as a stand-alone theme in the data we track, in 2008, 2016 and 2020.

25
00:01:37.362 --> 00:01:43.168
<v Tutor>And fluid responsiveness turns up inside many more shock questions as a single line of the marking.

26
00:01:43.168 --> 00:01:47.075
<v Tutor>Here's the 2016 second sitting, question 24, read word for word.

27
00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:53.097
<v Tutor>"With respect to haemodynamic monitoring in the critically ill patient: a, Define fluid responsiveness.

28
00:01:53.097 --> 00:02:03.352
<v Tutor>b, Outline the physiological basis and the limitations of the following methods of assessment of fluid responsiveness in a patient on mechanical ventilation: one, Passive leg raise.

29
00:02:03.352 --> 00:02:05.360
<v Tutor>Two, Central venous pressure.

30
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:16.620
<v Tutor>Three, Pulse pressure variation." The pass rate was 53 percent, and the examiners wrote that for a core topic, the overall understanding was lacking in a significant number of candidates.

31
00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.042
<v Tutor>Let's rebuild the answer that earns the marks.

32
00:02:20.042 --> 00:02:26.421
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 24, haemodynamic monitoring, and chapter 58, fluid and electrolyte therapy, are your sources.

33
00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:28.100
<v Tutor>First, the definition.

34
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:35.934
<v Tutor>The College answer defines fluid responsiveness as an increase in stroke volume, or cardiac output, of 10 to 15 percent after fluid.

35
00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:41.865
<v Tutor>And it adds the line that candidates forget: fluid responsiveness is not the same as hypovolaemia.

36
00:02:41.865 --> 00:02:44.327
<v Tutor>Healthy people are fluid responsive.

37
00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:50.314
<v Tutor>Being on the steep part of the Frank Starling curve tells you that fluid would raise stroke volume.

38
00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:53.056
<v Tutor>It doesn't tell you the patient needs it.

39
00:02:53.056 --> 00:02:55.518
<v Tutor>Second, the central venous pressure.

40
00:02:55.518 --> 00:03:01.730
<v Tutor>The physiology is that it estimates right atrial pressure, which is the back pressure to venous return.

41
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.158
<v Tutor>The limitation is that a single value predicts fluid response no better than a coin toss.

42
00:03:07.158 --> 00:03:14.880
<v Tutor>It is changed by intrathoracic pressure, PEEP, tricuspid regurgitation, right ventricular compliance and intra-abdominal pressure.

43
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.230
<v Tutor>Its real use is as a safety limit.

44
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:27.862
<v Tutor>A high and rising central venous pressure with no rise in output tells you the right heart has had enough, and venous congestion of the kidney is a real cause of acute kidney injury.

45
00:03:27.862 --> 00:03:30.100
<v Tutor>Third, pulse pressure variation.

46
00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:37.959
<v Tutor>In a ventilated patient, each positive pressure breath squeezes blood from the lungs into the left heart, then reduces right ventricular filling.

47
00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:44.082
<v Tutor>If both ventricles sit on the steep part of their curves, stroke volume and pulse pressure swing with each breath.

48
00:03:44.082 --> 00:03:50.456
<v Tutor>A variation above about 12 percent predicts response, with excellent accuracy in the conditions where it was validated.

49
00:03:50.456 --> 00:03:52.464
<v Tutor>But those conditions are narrow.

50
00:03:52.464 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Tutor>The patient must be fully passive on the ventilator, in sinus rhythm, with a tidal volume of at least 8 millilitres per kilogram, a closed chest, and normal intra-abdominal pressure.

51
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.011
<v Tutor>Right ventricular failure gives you false positives.

52
00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:07.320
<v Tutor>Low lung compliance blunts the signal.

53
00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.623
<v Tutor>So here's the first pause.

54
00:04:09.623 --> 00:04:16.956
<v Tutor>Our man is in atrial fibrillation, ventilated at six millilitres per kilogram, with a fresh laparotomy and a tense abdomen.

55
00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:23.225
<v Tutor>Name three reasons his pulse pressure variation is uninterpretable, and two tests you could use instead.

56
00:04:23.225 --> 00:04:24.680
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

57
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:36.674
<v Tutor>Three reasons: the arrhythmia makes beat to beat stroke volume vary for reasons that have nothing to do with ventilation.

58
00:04:36.674 --> 00:04:40.866
<v Tutor>The low tidal volume makes the swings in intrathoracic pressure too small.

59
00:04:40.866 --> 00:04:45.723
<v Tutor>And raised intra-abdominal pressure distorts the transmission of pressure to the heart.

60
00:04:45.723 --> 00:04:47.154
<v Tutor>What to use instead.

61
00:04:47.154 --> 00:04:53.391
<v Tutor>The passive leg raise, which moves about 300 millilitres of blood from the legs and splanchnic bed into the chest.

62
00:04:53.391 --> 00:04:56.151
<v Tutor>It's reversible, and it gives no fluid at all.

63
00:04:56.151 --> 00:05:03.359
<v Tutor>But it must be read with a real-time measure of cardiac output, such as the velocity time integral on echo or pulse contour analysis.

64
00:05:03.359 --> 00:05:06.528
<v Tutor>Blood pressure alone misses about half the responders.

65
00:05:06.528 --> 00:05:09.902
<v Tutor>A rise in output of 10 percent or more is a positive test.

66
00:05:09.902 --> 00:05:17.621
<v Tutor>It is unreliable with high intra-abdominal pressure, and you shouldn't do it with raised intracranial pressure, or with a fresh femoral sheath.

67
00:05:17.621 --> 00:05:19.768
<v Tutor>The end-expiratory occlusion test.

68
00:05:19.768 --> 00:05:22.887
<v Tutor>Hold the ventilator at end expiration for 15 seconds.

69
00:05:22.887 --> 00:05:29.175
<v Tutor>That removes the cyclic fall in venous return, and a rise in cardiac output of 5 percent or more predicts response.

70
00:05:29.175 --> 00:05:35.360
<v Tutor>It works with arrhythmias and low tidal volumes, as long as the patient can tolerate the hold without triggering.

71
00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.026
<v Tutor>Two more you can name for a top mark.

72
00:05:38.026 --> 00:05:48.801
<v Tutor>The tidal volume challenge: increase tidal volume from six to eight millilitres per kilogram for one minute, and a rise in pulse pressure variation of about 3.5 percent predicts response.

73
00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:55.818
<v Tutor>And the mini fluid challenge: 100 millilitres over a minute, with the velocity time integral measured before and after.

74
00:05:55.818 --> 00:05:59.519
<v Tutor>And the reference standard is still the fluid bolus itself.

75
00:05:59.519 --> 00:06:04.768
<v Tutor>Say 250 millilitres over ten minutes, with a pre-set target and a pre-set safety limit.

76
00:06:04.768 --> 00:06:07.421
<v Tutor>It's the only test that actually treats.

77
00:06:07.421 --> 00:06:12.780
<v Tutor>But it is irreversible, and repeated boluses are how patients end up ten litres positive.

78
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.214
<v Examiner>Let me push you, then.

79
00:06:15.214 --> 00:06:17.157
<v Examiner>Say the leg raise is positive.

80
00:06:17.157 --> 00:06:19.407
<v Examiner>His cardiac output rises 14 percent.

81
00:06:19.407 --> 00:06:22.066
<v Examiner>He's still oliguric, and he's six litres up.

82
00:06:22.066 --> 00:06:23.600
<v Examiner>Do you give the fluid?

83
00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:29.851
<v Candidate>I think I'd give a small bolus, because he's responsive and his kidneys are hypoperfused.

84
00:06:29.851 --> 00:06:34.060
<v Candidate>Maybe 250 millilitres of a balanced crystalloid, then reassess.

85
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.514
<v Examiner>That's the answer most candidates give.

86
00:06:36.514 --> 00:06:37.720
<v Examiner>What did you not ask?

87
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.162
<v Candidate>Whether he needs a higher cardiac output at all.

88
00:06:41.162 --> 00:06:48.728
<v Candidate>If his lactate is clearing, his capillary refill is normal, and his venous saturation is fine, then responsiveness alone isn't a reason.

89
00:06:48.728 --> 00:06:50.987
<v Candidate>And I didn't look at the harm side.

90
00:06:50.987 --> 00:06:56.241
<v Candidate>He's in a positive balance, his abdomen is tense, and his central venous pressure is rising.

91
00:06:56.241 --> 00:07:01.337
<v Candidate>Venous congestion and intra-abdominal hypertension can both be driving the kidney injury.

92
00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:09.060
<v Candidate>So I'd measure bladder pressure first, and I'd think about raising the noradrenaline, or adding vasopressin, rather than giving more fluid.

93
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:10.227
<v Examiner>Good.

94
00:07:10.227 --> 00:07:12.895
<v Examiner>That second answer is the consultant answer.

95
00:07:12.895 --> 00:07:14.639
<v Examiner>Three questions, in order.

96
00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:16.230
<v Examiner>Is there hypoperfusion?

97
00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.461
<v Examiner>Will fluid help?

98
00:07:17.461 --> 00:07:18.795
<v Examiner>And is fluid safe?

99
00:07:18.795 --> 00:07:21.720
<v Examiner>A positive test answers only the middle question.

100
00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.590
<v Tutor>That brings in the evidence on how much fluid to give.

101
00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:29.088
<v Tutor>Two recent trials set the tone.

102
00:07:29.088 --> 00:07:39.654
<v Tutor>CLASSIC, in the New England Journal in 2022, restricted fluid after initial resuscitation in septic shock, and 90-day mortality was 42.3 versus 42.1 percent.

103
00:07:39.654 --> 00:07:41.063
<v Tutor>No difference.

104
00:07:41.063 --> 00:07:54.255
<v Tutor>CLOVERS, in 2023, compared a restrictive, early vasopressor strategy with a liberal fluid strategy in sepsis induced hypotension, and 90-day death before discharge home was 14.0 versus 14.9 percent.

105
00:07:54.255 --> 00:07:56.112
<v Tutor>Again, no difference.

106
00:07:56.112 --> 00:08:07.062
<v Tutor>The take-home for an exam answer: after the first resuscitation, less fluid and earlier vasopressors is at least as safe, so the burden of proof is on giving more.

107
00:08:07.062 --> 00:08:12.314
<v Tutor>Since we've raised vasopressin, here's something new on this week's theme.

108
00:08:12.314 --> 00:08:20.831
<v Tutor>VASSPR, published in JAMA on the 14th of October, is a pragmatic cluster crossover trial from 13 hospitals in Utah and Idaho.

109
00:08:20.831 --> 00:08:23.456
<v Tutor>3,959 patients with septic shock.

110
00:08:23.456 --> 00:08:36.520
<v Tutor>Hospitals switched month by month between starting vasopressin at 0.03 units per minute when the noradrenaline-equivalent dose reached 0.1 micrograms per kilogram per minute, or waiting until 0.4.

111
00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:43.180
<v Tutor>28-day mortality was 31.8 percent with the lower threshold and 33.0 percent with the higher one.

112
00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.483
<v Tutor>Not significantly different.

113
00:08:45.483 --> 00:08:52.326
<v Tutor>Persistent or new cardiogenic shock was a little more common with early vasopressin, 7.4 versus 6.3 percent.

114
00:08:52.326 --> 00:09:03.120
<v Tutor>How to say it in an answer: early vasopressin is a reasonable noradrenaline-sparing strategy, but neither VANISH nor VASSPR shows a mortality benefit from starting it earlier.

115
00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:12.365
<v Tutor>Now, a story, because this week's landmark reading is the CHEST trial, and the story behind it is one of the strangest in our specialty.

116
00:09:12.365 --> 00:09:15.851
<v Tutor>Hydroxyethyl starch is made from maize or potato starch.

117
00:09:15.851 --> 00:09:22.171
<v Tutor>The glucose chains are tagged with hydroxyethyl groups so that plasma amylase can't break them down quickly.

118
00:09:22.171 --> 00:09:23.750
<v Tutor>The idea was elegant.

119
00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:30.723
<v Tutor>Give a big molecule, and it stays in the vessels, so you need less volume than with crystalloid to fill the circulation.

120
00:09:30.723 --> 00:09:35.844
<v Tutor>For decades it was routine in theatres across Europe and in many intensive care units.

121
00:09:35.844 --> 00:09:43.144
<v Tutor>Much of the published evidence that starches were safe, and even protective, came from one German anaesthetist, Joachim Boldt.

122
00:09:43.144 --> 00:09:47.285
<v Tutor>In November 2010 he was suspended from his hospital in Ludwigshafen.

123
00:09:47.285 --> 00:09:53.822
<v Tutor>In March 2011, the editors of sixteen journals said that 89 of 102 of his studies lacked proper ethics approval.

124
00:09:53.822 --> 00:09:57.635
<v Tutor>Retractions followed in waves, and they are still being added.

125
00:09:57.635 --> 00:10:01.612
<v Tutor>A whole evidence base for a fluid had a hole in the middle of it.

126
00:10:01.612 --> 00:10:04.608
<v Tutor>Meanwhile, two independent trials were running.

127
00:10:04.608 --> 00:10:11.200
<v Tutor>In July 2012, the Scandinavian 6S trial compared starch with Ringer's acetate in 804 patients with severe sepsis.

128
00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:18.174
<v Tutor>Death or dialysis dependence at 90 days was 51 versus 43 percent, a relative risk of 1.17.

129
00:10:18.174 --> 00:10:22.264
<v Tutor>Renal replacement therapy was 22 versus 16 percent.

130
00:10:22.264 --> 00:10:27.740
<v Tutor>Then in October 2012, from our own ANZICS Clinical Trials Group, CHEST.

131
00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.342
<v Tutor>CHEST, Myburgh and colleagues, New England Journal of Medicine, 2012.

132
00:10:33.342 --> 00:10:40.503
<v Tutor>7,000 adults in 32 Australian and New Zealand intensive care units who needed fluid resuscitation.

133
00:10:40.503 --> 00:10:49.353
<v Tutor>Six percent hydroxyethyl starch 130 over 0.4 versus 0.9 percent saline, blinded, up to 50 millilitres per kilogram per day.

134
00:10:49.353 --> 00:10:58.406
<v Tutor>Primary outcome, 90-day mortality: 18.0 versus 17.0 percent, relative risk 1.06, with a confidence interval from 0.96 to 1.18.

135
00:10:58.406 --> 00:11:00.501
<v Tutor>No difference in death.

136
00:11:00.501 --> 00:11:08.540
<v Tutor>But renal replacement therapy was 7.0 versus 5.8 percent, relative risk 1.21, confidence interval 1.00 to 1.45.

137
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.837
<v Tutor>More dialysis with starch.

138
00:11:10.837 --> 00:11:20.295
<v Tutor>And starch reduced the volume of resuscitation fluid only modestly, about 526 versus 616 millilitres a day over the first four days.

139
00:11:20.295 --> 00:11:22.998
<v Tutor>Nowhere near the promised ratio.

140
00:11:22.998 --> 00:11:24.889
<v Tutor>The main criticisms.

141
00:11:24.889 --> 00:11:29.551
<v Tutor>About 15 percent of patients had starch before randomisation.

142
00:11:29.551 --> 00:11:34.821
<v Tutor>Mortality, 17 to 18 percent, was lower than the trial had planned for.

143
00:11:34.821 --> 00:11:41.239
<v Tutor>And many patients were not badly shocked at enrolment, with mean base excess near zero.

144
00:11:41.239 --> 00:11:42.860
<v Tutor>What it changed.

145
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:54.854
<v Tutor>In June 2013, Europe's safety committee recommended suspending starch, and the American regulator added a boxed warning that year, advising against starch in critically ill adults including sepsis.

146
00:11:54.854 --> 00:12:00.501
<v Tutor>In February 2022 the European committee recommended suspension from the market altogether.

147
00:12:00.501 --> 00:12:06.725
<v Tutor>The Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 makes a strong recommendation against starches for resuscitation.

148
00:12:06.725 --> 00:12:19.173
<v Tutor>How to quote it in an SAQ: "Starches increase renal replacement therapy without reducing mortality: CHEST, 7,000 patients, relative risk for dialysis 1.21, and 6S showed increased death or dialysis in sepsis.

149
00:12:19.173 --> 00:12:28.047
<v Tutor>I would not use them." The lesson that examiners like: physiology predicted a benefit, small trials agreed, and large pragmatic trials found harm.

150
00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:32.600
<v Tutor>Which is why your answers should lean on outcome trials, not mechanism.

151
00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.105
<v Tutor>Second pause.

152
00:12:34.105 --> 00:12:42.472
<v Tutor>Without looking: in CHEST, what were the rates of renal replacement therapy with starch and with saline, and what was the 90-day mortality in each arm?

153
00:12:42.472 --> 00:12:43.840
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

154
00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:52.755
<v Tutor>Renal replacement therapy, 7.0 versus 5.8 percent.

155
00:12:52.755 --> 00:12:57.725
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality, 18.0 versus 17.0 percent, which was not significant.

156
00:12:57.725 --> 00:13:01.361
<v Tutor>One sentence: no survival difference, more dialysis.

157
00:13:01.361 --> 00:13:04.937
<v Tutor>The overlap with the kidney that makes this harder.

158
00:13:04.937 --> 00:13:10.997
<v Tutor>If our man goes on to continuous renal replacement therapy, his fluid question turns around.

159
00:13:10.997 --> 00:13:15.603
<v Tutor>You now control fluid removal by setting a net ultrafiltration rate.

160
00:13:15.603 --> 00:13:23.178
<v Tutor>A common starting point is 1 to 2 millilitres per kilogram per hour, titrated against perfusion and vasopressor dose.

161
00:13:23.178 --> 00:13:33.360
<v Tutor>That is de-resuscitation, and it's the fourth phase of fluid therapy that the College expects you to name: rescue, optimisation, stabilisation, then evacuation.

162
00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:35.854
<v Tutor>Revisit two.

163
00:13:35.854 --> 00:13:37.221
<v Tutor>Three weeks back.

164
00:13:37.221 --> 00:13:40.667
<v Tutor>Community-acquired pneumonia that isn't getting better.

165
00:13:40.667 --> 00:13:46.903
<v Tutor>You scored five and a half on this question on the 12th of October, and five and a half again on the 16th.

166
00:13:46.903 --> 00:13:48.872
<v Tutor>Both times part a was solid.

167
00:13:48.872 --> 00:13:51.935
<v Tutor>Part b, the assessment, is where the marks went.

168
00:13:51.935 --> 00:13:59.155
<v Tutor>If you haven't written today's attempt yet, this would be a good moment to stop the episode and do it first, then come back.

169
00:13:59.155 --> 00:14:08.180
<v Tutor>William Osler, in his textbook at the turn of the twentieth century, borrowed a phrase from John Bunyan and called pneumonia the captain of the men of death.

170
00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:14.306
<v Tutor>He also called it the old man's friend, because it brought a quick and fairly peaceful end to the frail.

171
00:14:14.306 --> 00:14:23.276
<v Tutor>A century and a half of antibiotics later, severe pneumonia still carries a hospital mortality that would have been familiar to him in the sickest patients.

172
00:14:23.276 --> 00:14:26.175
<v Tutor>That's why the College keeps asking about it.

173
00:14:26.175 --> 00:14:30.004
<v Tutor>Here's the question, from the 2025 first sitting, question 11.

174
00:14:30.004 --> 00:14:36.294
<v Tutor>"A 67-year-old patient is admitted to the ICU with a presumptive diagnosis of community-acquired pneumonia.

175
00:14:36.294 --> 00:14:41.600
<v Tutor>On day 5 they remain intubated and ventilated for persistent hypoxic respiratory failure.

176
00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:46.958
<v Tutor>a, Outline six factors that may predict a poor response to antibiotics in CAP.

177
00:14:46.958 --> 00:14:47.922
<v Tutor>3 marks.

178
00:14:47.922 --> 00:14:55.448
<v Tutor>b, Outline your assessment of this patient with respiratory failure that is refractory to standard treatment for CAP.

179
00:14:55.448 --> 00:14:58.398
<v Tutor>7 marks." The pass rate was 58.7 percent.

180
00:14:58.398 --> 00:15:05.925
<v Tutor>Community-acquired pneumonia has come up four times in the questions we track, in 2012 twice, 2025 and again in 2026.

181
00:15:05.925 --> 00:15:09.899
<v Tutor>The examiners said that part b was an assessment question.

182
00:15:09.899 --> 00:15:20.978
<v Tutor>They wanted a focused history, examination and investigations aimed at the causes of non-resolving pneumonia, and at the non-infective causes of refractory respiratory failure.

183
00:15:20.978 --> 00:15:27.300
<v Tutor>And they said that bullet points for a complete investigation list were acceptable and efficient.

184
00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.599
<v Tutor>Third pause, and this is your part a.

185
00:15:30.599 --> 00:15:36.785
<v Tutor>Six factors that predict a poor response to antibiotics in community-acquired pneumonia.

186
00:15:36.785 --> 00:15:38.460
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

187
00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:47.286
<v Tutor>Here's a list that would score all three marks.

188
00:15:47.286 --> 00:15:50.214
<v Tutor>Think host, bug, disease and treatment.

189
00:15:50.214 --> 00:15:56.132
<v Tutor>Host: age over 65, immunosuppression, and chronic lung or liver disease or alcohol use.

190
00:15:56.132 --> 00:15:59.122
<v Tutor>Bug: a resistant or unexpected organism.

191
00:15:59.122 --> 00:16:08.840
<v Tutor>Staphylococcus aureus including MRSA, Pseudomonas, Legionella, or an organism the empiric cover misses, such as tuberculosis, fungi or Pneumocystis.

192
00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:15.630
<v Tutor>Disease: multilobar involvement, bacteraemia, septic shock at presentation, or a high severity score.

193
00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:19.679
<v Tutor>Complications: an empyema, a lung abscess, or cavitation.

194
00:16:19.679 --> 00:16:24.912
<v Tutor>Treatment: delayed or inappropriate first antibiotics, or inadequate dosing.

195
00:16:24.912 --> 00:16:27.964
<v Tutor>That's more than six, which is the point.

196
00:16:27.964 --> 00:16:30.020
<v Tutor>Write six, one line each.

197
00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:32.491
<v Tutor>Now part b, the seven marks.

198
00:16:32.491 --> 00:16:36.748
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 48, pneumonia, frames non-resolution in four buckets.

199
00:16:36.748 --> 00:16:40.602
<v Tutor>Use them as your structure, and you'll stop missing things.

200
00:16:40.602 --> 00:16:45.377
<v Tutor>Bucket one: it's the right diagnosis, but the wrong drug or the wrong dose.

201
00:16:45.377 --> 00:16:50.727
<v Tutor>A resistant organism, an atypical one, or an antibiotic that isn't reaching the lung.

202
00:16:50.727 --> 00:16:56.365
<v Tutor>Think about dosing in a patient with augmented clearance, or on renal replacement therapy.

203
00:16:56.365 --> 00:17:00.565
<v Tutor>Bucket two: it's the right diagnosis, but there's a complication.

204
00:17:00.565 --> 00:17:05.800
<v Tutor>Empyema, abscess, a new ventilator-associated pneumonia, a line infection, or ARDS.

205
00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:08.734
<v Tutor>Bucket three: it's not an infection at all.

206
00:17:08.734 --> 00:17:09.827
<v Tutor>The mimics.

207
00:17:09.827 --> 00:17:11.955
<v Tutor>Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.

208
00:17:11.955 --> 00:17:13.509
<v Tutor>Pulmonary embolism.

209
00:17:13.509 --> 00:17:16.097
<v Tutor>Vasculitis with alveolar haemorrhage.

210
00:17:16.097 --> 00:17:17.766
<v Tutor>Organising pneumonia.

211
00:17:17.766 --> 00:17:19.894
<v Tutor>Acute eosinophilic pneumonia.

212
00:17:19.894 --> 00:17:21.850
<v Tutor>Drug-induced lung disease.

213
00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:23.461
<v Tutor>Lymphangitic cancer.

214
00:17:23.461 --> 00:17:27.603
<v Tutor>Bucket four: it's an infection, but not the one you're treating.

215
00:17:27.603 --> 00:17:32.953
<v Tutor>Tuberculosis, fungi, Pneumocystis, viruses, and the Australian zoonoses like Q fever.

216
00:17:32.953 --> 00:17:38.361
<v Tutor>Then, for the marks, run history, examination and investigations across those buckets.

217
00:17:38.361 --> 00:17:41.525
<v Tutor>History: travel, occupation and animal contact.

218
00:17:41.525 --> 00:17:47.220
<v Tutor>Rash, haemoptysis, joint pain and renal symptoms, which point to connective tissue disease.

219
00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:51.762
<v Tutor>Drugs, including amiodarone, methotrexate and nitrofurantoin.

220
00:17:51.762 --> 00:17:54.797
<v Tutor>Immune status, HIV risk and steroid use.

221
00:17:54.797 --> 00:18:05.608
<v Tutor>Examination: rash, synovitis, an effusion, fluid status, cardiac signs and murmurs, line sites, and a review of the ventilator and the chest drain if there is one.

222
00:18:05.608 --> 00:18:12.878
<v Tutor>Investigations, and this is the list you were missing: repeat blood and sputum cultures with sensitivities.

223
00:18:12.878 --> 00:18:19.453
<v Tutor>Bronchoalveolar lavage for resistant organisms, fungi, Pneumocystis, mycobacteria and viral PCR.

224
00:18:19.453 --> 00:18:25.333
<v Tutor>Legionella and pneumococcal urinary antigens, and serology for atypicals and Q fever.

225
00:18:25.333 --> 00:18:28.936
<v Tutor>A QuantiFERON, or interferon gamma release assay.

226
00:18:28.936 --> 00:18:37.281
<v Tutor>An autoimmune screen: ANCA, anti-GBM antibodies, ANA, complement and CK, and a urine sediment for active glomerulonephritis.

227
00:18:37.281 --> 00:18:40.063
<v Tutor>An echo and BNP for cardiac failure.

228
00:18:40.063 --> 00:18:49.040
<v Tutor>A CT pulmonary angiogram, or high resolution CT, which will show abscess, empyema, cavitation and the pattern of organising pneumonia.

229
00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:50.684
<v Tutor>Antibiotic levels.

230
00:18:50.684 --> 00:18:54.920
<v Tutor>And finally, lung biopsy if the diagnosis is still unclear.

231
00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.803
<v Examiner>And the harder version.

232
00:18:57.803 --> 00:18:59.147
<v Examiner>Same patient.

233
00:18:59.147 --> 00:19:04.329
<v Examiner>The lavage is bloodstained, and gets more bloodstained with each aliquot.

234
00:19:04.329 --> 00:19:08.295
<v Examiner>Haemoglobin has fallen from 120 to 85 grams per litre.

235
00:19:08.295 --> 00:19:14.309
<v Examiner>Creatinine is 280 micromoles per litre and the urine dipstick shows blood and protein.

236
00:19:14.309 --> 00:19:17.380
<v Examiner>What's your diagnosis, and what changes?

237
00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:21.823
<v Candidate>That's diffuse alveolar haemorrhage with a renal lesion.

238
00:19:21.823 --> 00:19:23.980
<v Candidate>A pulmonary-renal syndrome.

239
00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:28.355
<v Candidate>The most likely causes are ANCA vasculitis or anti-GBM disease.

240
00:19:28.355 --> 00:19:34.455
<v Candidate>I'd send ANCA and anti-GBM urgently, look for red cell casts, and ask renal about a biopsy.

241
00:19:34.455 --> 00:19:41.233
<v Candidate>And I'd start methylprednisolone, about 1 gram a day for three days, without waiting for the serology.

242
00:19:41.233 --> 00:19:44.560
<v Candidate>Then plasma exchange if it's anti-GBM disease.

243
00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:45.570
<v Examiner>Good.

244
00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.060
<v Examiner>Where would you lose the mark?

245
00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:53.407
<v Candidate>By not checking coagulation and platelets first, and by not asking whether he's been on anticoagulants.

246
00:19:53.407 --> 00:19:59.360
<v Candidate>And by forgetting that infection can still cause alveolar haemorrhage, so I'd keep the antibiotics going.

247
00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:00.689
<v Examiner>Right.

248
00:20:00.689 --> 00:20:09.098
<v Examiner>Plasma exchange for ANCA vasculitis is now a selective decision after the PEXIVAS trial, but for anti-GBM disease it remains standard.

249
00:20:09.098 --> 00:20:11.940
<v Examiner>That nuance is a consultant-level point.

250
00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:16.352
<v Tutor>One more piece, because it changes management.

251
00:20:16.352 --> 00:20:29.748
<v Tutor>The 2023 European severe pneumonia guideline, from the ERS, ESICM, ESCMID and ALAT, makes a conditional recommendation for corticosteroids in severe community-acquired pneumonia when there is shock.

252
00:20:29.748 --> 00:20:36.967
<v Tutor>The trial that has moved the field since is CAPE COD, Dequin and colleagues, New England Journal, 2023.

253
00:20:36.967 --> 00:20:41.975
<v Tutor>800 patients in 31 French intensive care units with severe pneumonia.

254
00:20:41.975 --> 00:20:48.283
<v Tutor>Hydrocortisone 200 milligrams a day for four days, then tapered to 8 or 14 days in total.

255
00:20:48.283 --> 00:20:54.266
<v Tutor>28-day mortality was 6.2 versus 11.9 percent, an absolute difference of 5.6 percent.

256
00:20:54.266 --> 00:20:57.387
<v Tutor>Intubation was 18.0 versus 29.5 percent.

257
00:20:57.387 --> 00:20:58.688
<v Tutor>Two catches.

258
00:20:58.688 --> 00:21:04.801
<v Tutor>Patients with septic shock at enrolment were excluded, and influenza was excluded too.

259
00:21:04.801 --> 00:21:12.800
<v Tutor>So quote it as: "hydrocortisone reduced 28-day mortality in severe non-influenza pneumonia without shock at entry."

260
00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:23.229
<v Tutor>Two more things that turn a pass into a strong pass on pneumonia, because the 2026 first sitting asked about it again, and the pass rate there was 71.8 percent.

261
00:21:23.229 --> 00:21:28.601
<v Tutor>The candidates who knew the severity criteria and the antibiotic choices did well.

262
00:21:28.601 --> 00:21:29.616
<v Tutor>Severity.

263
00:21:29.616 --> 00:21:44.898
<v Tutor>The 2019 American Thoracic Society and Infectious Diseases Society guideline defines severe pneumonia by one major criterion, which is septic shock needing vasopressors or respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation, or three minor criteria.

264
00:21:44.898 --> 00:22:02.150
<v Tutor>The minor criteria are: respiratory rate of 30 or more, a PaO2 to FiO2 ratio of 250 or less, multilobar infiltrates, confusion, urea of 7 millimoles per litre or more, white cells below 4, platelets below 100, temperature below 36 degrees, and hypotension needing aggressive fluid.

265
00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:11.702
<v Tutor>In Australia, SMART-COP is the other score worth naming, because it was built here to predict the need for intensive respiratory or vasopressor support.

266
00:22:11.702 --> 00:22:12.895
<v Tutor>Antibiotics.

267
00:22:12.895 --> 00:22:23.760
<v Tutor>Following Australian practice for severe pneumonia, a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone, plus a macrolide such as azithromycin, which also covers Legionella.

268
00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:34.900
<v Tutor>Add cover for Staphylococcus aureus if there are risk factors, such as recent influenza or a cavitating picture, and for Pseudomonas if there is structural lung disease or recent hospital exposure.

269
00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:37.520
<v Tutor>Then narrow as soon as you have a result.

270
00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:51.260
<v Tutor>And the history line that earns marks in Australia: ask about melioidosis risk in the tropical north, especially with diabetes, alcohol use or chronic kidney disease, because Burkholderia pseudomallei needs meropenem and is missed by standard cover.

271
00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:53.923
<v Tutor>Revisit three.

272
00:22:53.923 --> 00:22:56.104
<v Tutor>Lactate after cardiac surgery.

273
00:22:56.104 --> 00:23:02.417
<v Tutor>You met this three weeks ago, and scored five and a half on the 13th of October, then six on the 16th.

274
00:23:02.417 --> 00:23:06.607
<v Tutor>That's retired, but the theme is one of the College's favourites.

275
00:23:06.607 --> 00:23:12.519
<v Tutor>Causes of lactic acidosis appear in thirteen questions in the data we track, from 2008 to 2021.

276
00:23:12.519 --> 00:23:15.503
<v Tutor>Here's the 2018 second sitting, question 12.

277
00:23:15.503 --> 00:23:21.702
<v Tutor>"List the causes of an elevated lactate immediately following an aortic valve replacement procedure.

278
00:23:21.702 --> 00:23:26.753
<v Tutor>Outline your approach to determining the cause." The pass rate was 46.3 percent.

279
00:23:26.753 --> 00:23:35.879
<v Tutor>And the examiner comment matters: many candidates gave a general list of causes of high lactate, rather than causes specific to the post-valve setting.

280
00:23:35.879 --> 00:23:40.298
<v Tutor>Some outlined management instead of an approach to finding the cause.

281
00:23:40.298 --> 00:23:43.111
<v Tutor>So the answer has to be setting-specific.

282
00:23:43.111 --> 00:23:50.170
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 57, acid-base disorders and hyperlactataemia, gives the framework, and the College answer fills it in.

283
00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:54.934
<v Tutor>Before surgery: drugs, especially metformin, linezolid and antiretrovirals.

284
00:23:54.934 --> 00:24:01.420
<v Tutor>On bypass: a long bypass run, a lactate-containing prime, inadequate pump flow and prolonged hypothermia.

285
00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:08.608
<v Tutor>After surgery, low cardiac output: tamponade, myocardial ischaemia or infarction, and a malfunctioning valve.

286
00:24:08.608 --> 00:24:12.022
<v Tutor>Then: splanchnic ischaemia and liver dysfunction.

287
00:24:12.022 --> 00:24:20.528
<v Tutor>And the others: beta agonists, especially adrenaline, measurement error, ischaemic muscle and rhabdomyolysis, and thiamine deficiency.

288
00:24:20.528 --> 00:24:22.624
<v Tutor>Your approach then follows.

289
00:24:22.624 --> 00:24:25.799
<v Tutor>History of the bypass run and the drug chart.

290
00:24:25.799 --> 00:24:31.549
<v Tutor>Examination of perfusion, infusions, temperature, the drains, the abdomen and the limbs.

291
00:24:31.549 --> 00:24:48.800
<v Tutor>Investigations: repeat the lactate, a blood gas with a venous saturation, full blood count, coagulation and biochemistry with CK, a chest film, an echo looking for tamponade and valve function, imaging of the abdomen, and red cell transketolase if thiamine deficiency is possible.

292
00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:56.022
<v Tutor>Before the gas, the reasoning behind why we chase lactate at all, because it explains several exam answers.

293
00:24:56.022 --> 00:24:59.522
<v Tutor>Lactate isn't a waste product and it isn't a toxin.

294
00:24:59.522 --> 00:25:02.310
<v Tutor>Every cell makes pyruvate from glucose.

295
00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:12.098
<v Tutor>When pyruvate is made faster than the mitochondria can use it, it's converted to lactate, which regenerates the cofactor that glycolysis needs to keep going.

296
00:25:12.098 --> 00:25:17.081
<v Tutor>The liver then takes up most of that lactate and turns it back into glucose.

297
00:25:17.081 --> 00:25:23.013
<v Tutor>That's the Cori cycle, described by Carl and Gerty Cori, who shared the Nobel Prize in 1947.

298
00:25:23.013 --> 00:25:26.632
<v Tutor>So a high lactate means production exceeds clearance.

299
00:25:26.632 --> 00:25:35.115
<v Tutor>Production rises with tissue hypoxia, which is type A, and also with adrenaline, sepsis-driven glycolysis, and drugs, which are type B.

300
00:25:35.115 --> 00:25:38.674
<v Tutor>Clearance falls with liver hypoperfusion or failure.

301
00:25:38.674 --> 00:25:45.140
<v Tutor>That's why the post-valve answer has to include the liver and the splanchnic bed, not just the heart.

302
00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:48.521
<v Tutor>And it's why lactate is a slow, imperfect target.

303
00:25:48.521 --> 00:25:58.013
<v Tutor>ANDROMEDA-SHOCK, in JAMA in 2019, randomised 424 patients with early septic shock to resuscitation targeting capillary refill time or targeting lactate.

304
00:25:58.013 --> 00:26:02.640
<v Tutor>28-day mortality was 34.9 versus 43.4 percent, with a p value of 0.06.

305
00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:09.422
<v Tutor>Not statistically significant, but capillary refill was at least as good, and it responds in minutes, not hours.

306
00:26:09.422 --> 00:26:16.300
<v Tutor>That's why the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 suggests capillary refill as an adjunct to other measures of perfusion.

307
00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:17.862
<v Tutor>Now harder.

308
00:26:17.862 --> 00:26:23.428
<v Tutor>Two hours after an aortic valve replacement, the patient is sedated and ventilated.

309
00:26:23.428 --> 00:26:31.256
<v Tutor>The gas shows a pH of 7.19, a carbon dioxide of 38 millimetres of mercury, and a bicarbonate of 14 millimoles per litre.

310
00:26:31.256 --> 00:26:37.800
<v Tutor>Sodium 136 millimoles per litre, chloride 102 millimoles per litre, and albumin 28 grams per litre.

311
00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:40.369
<v Tutor>Lactate is 9 millimoles per litre.

312
00:26:40.369 --> 00:26:47.525
<v Tutor>He's on adrenaline at 0.15 micrograms per kilogram per minute, and his mixed venous saturation is 72 percent.

313
00:26:47.525 --> 00:26:56.210
<v Tutor>This is where marks were lost on the 20th of October, on question 26 from the 2013 second sitting, which you scored one and a half on.

314
00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.839
<v Tutor>The compensation formula was wrong.

315
00:26:58.839 --> 00:27:00.980
<v Tutor>So let's make it automatic.

316
00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:02.546
<v Tutor>Fourth pause.

317
00:27:02.546 --> 00:27:05.099
<v Tutor>Work this gas with the College method.

318
00:27:05.099 --> 00:27:12.536
<v Tutor>Calculate the anion gap without potassium, correct it for albumin, calculate the delta ratio, and the expected carbon dioxide.

319
00:27:12.536 --> 00:27:14.257
<v Tutor>What are the disorders?

320
00:27:14.257 --> 00:27:15.700
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

321
00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:25.250
<v Tutor>Anion gap: 136 minus the sum of 102 and 14, which is 20.

322
00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:30.049
<v Tutor>Albumin correction: add a quarter of the albumin deficit below 40.

323
00:27:30.049 --> 00:27:34.330
<v Tutor>That's 40 minus 28, which is 12, and a quarter of 12 is 3.

324
00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:36.405
<v Tutor>Corrected anion gap, 23.

325
00:27:36.405 --> 00:27:39.453
<v Tutor>Against a normal of 12, the rise is 11.

326
00:27:39.453 --> 00:27:43.279
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate has fallen from 24 to 14, a fall of 10.

327
00:27:43.279 --> 00:27:46.067
<v Tutor>Delta ratio, 11 over 10, about 1.1.

328
00:27:46.067 --> 00:27:55.341
<v Tutor>Between 1 and 2, so this is a pure high anion gap metabolic acidosis, with no hidden normal anion gap acidosis and no hidden alkalosis.

329
00:27:55.341 --> 00:28:02.669
<v Tutor>Expected carbon dioxide, Winters' formula: one and a half times the bicarbonate, plus 8, plus or minus 2.

330
00:28:02.669 --> 00:28:06.041
<v Tutor>One and a half times 14 is 21, plus 8 is 29.

331
00:28:06.041 --> 00:28:08.765
<v Tutor>So the expected range is 27 to 31.

332
00:28:08.765 --> 00:28:09.932
<v Tutor>His is 38.

333
00:28:09.932 --> 00:28:13.110
<v Tutor>That's a concurrent respiratory acidosis.

334
00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:19.141
<v Tutor>He's under-ventilated for this degree of acidosis, so turn up his minute ventilation.

335
00:28:19.141 --> 00:28:20.568
<v Tutor>And the twist.

336
00:28:20.568 --> 00:28:25.821
<v Tutor>With a venous saturation of 72 percent, he isn't obviously in low output.

337
00:28:25.821 --> 00:28:35.159
<v Tutor>Adrenaline stimulates beta 2 receptors on muscle, speeds up aerobic glycolysis, and pours out lactate even when oxygen delivery is fine.

338
00:28:35.159 --> 00:28:37.105
<v Tutor>That's type B lactate.

339
00:28:37.105 --> 00:28:39.180
<v Tutor>But you don't assume it.

340
00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:49.868
<v Tutor>You exclude tamponade and ischaemia with an echo and an ECG, look at the abdomen, and only then consider a switch to noradrenaline with an inodilator, and watch whether the lactate falls.

341
00:28:49.868 --> 00:28:54.880
<v Tutor>That is the setting-specific, data-based answer that scores seven or eight, not five.

342
00:28:55.780 --> 00:29:00.471
<v Examiner>One more twist, because examiners love to join the kidney to this.

343
00:29:00.471 --> 00:29:02.879
<v Examiner>Same day, a different patient.

344
00:29:02.879 --> 00:29:08.711
<v Examiner>A 72-year-old with type 2 diabetes on metformin, two days of vomiting, now oliguric.

345
00:29:08.711 --> 00:29:11.500
<v Examiner>Creatinine 640 micromoles per litre.

346
00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:16.888
<v Examiner>pH 6.95, bicarbonate 4 millimoles per litre, lactate 18 millimoles per litre.

347
00:29:16.888 --> 00:29:20.120
<v Examiner>Blood pressure 85 over 50 on noradrenaline.

348
00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:23.860
<v Examiner>What's going on, and what's the specific treatment?

349
00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.927
<v Candidate>Metformin-associated lactic acidosis.

350
00:29:26.927 --> 00:29:36.340
<v Candidate>Metformin is renally cleared, so with acute kidney injury it accumulates, and it inhibits mitochondrial respiration and hepatic gluconeogenesis from lactate.

351
00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:43.641
<v Candidate>But I'd still look for a second cause, like mesenteric ischaemia or sepsis, because the lactate could be type A as well.

352
00:29:43.641 --> 00:29:46.893
<v Candidate>The specific treatment is extracorporeal removal.

353
00:29:46.893 --> 00:29:57.561
<v Candidate>Metformin is small and water soluble, so intermittent haemodialysis clears it best, but if the patient is unstable I'd use continuous haemodiafiltration with a high effluent dose.

354
00:29:57.561 --> 00:30:08.000
<v Candidate>The extracorporeal treatments in poisoning workgroup recommends extracorporeal treatment when lactate is above 20, or the pH is 7.0 or lower, or when standard care is failing.

355
00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:09.048
<v Examiner>Good.

356
00:30:09.048 --> 00:30:10.060
<v Examiner>And bicarbonate?

357
00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:14.471
<v Candidate>Only as a bridge for severe acidaemia while dialysis is set up.

358
00:30:14.471 --> 00:30:20.200
<v Candidate>On its own it doesn't remove the metformin, and the extra carbon dioxide has to be breathed off.

359
00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.826
<v Examiner>That's a seven out of ten answer.

360
00:30:22.826 --> 00:30:29.100
<v Examiner>The eight comes from saying that a metformin level is useful to confirm the diagnosis, but you don't wait for it.

361
00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:31.810
<v Tutor>Journal watch.

362
00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:32.763
<v Tutor>PELICAN.

363
00:30:32.763 --> 00:30:34.906
<v Tutor>Here's the question it asks.

364
00:30:34.906 --> 00:30:41.575
<v Tutor>If you know what a patient's normal cardiac output is, should you defend that number during big surgery?

365
00:30:41.575 --> 00:30:48.065
<v Tutor>PELICAN was flagged by Critical Care Reviews this week, and published in JAMA on the 19th of October.

366
00:30:48.065 --> 00:30:50.090
<v Tutor>First author Bernd Saugel.

367
00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:55.627
<v Tutor>Design: a randomised trial across 12 university hospitals in five European countries.

368
00:30:55.627 --> 00:31:02.296
<v Tutor>1,111 high-risk adults having elective major abdominal surgery were randomised, and 1,095 were analysed.

369
00:31:02.296 --> 00:31:03.606
<v Tutor>Median age 67.

370
00:31:03.606 --> 00:31:11.168
<v Tutor>Intervention: haemodynamic therapy individualised to each patient's own resting cardiac index, measured before surgery.

371
00:31:11.168 --> 00:31:17.539
<v Tutor>Comparator: routine haemodynamic care, with cardiac index monitored but hidden from the clinicians.

372
00:31:17.539 --> 00:31:22.183
<v Tutor>Primary outcome: a composite of major complications within seven days.

373
00:31:22.183 --> 00:31:28.197
<v Tutor>Acute kidney injury, myocardial injury, severe infection, non-fatal cardiac arrest, or death.

374
00:31:28.197 --> 00:31:34.092
<v Tutor>It occurred in 44.3 percent with individualised therapy and 46.9 percent with routine care.

375
00:31:34.092 --> 00:31:36.593
<v Tutor>Relative risk 0.94, p equals 0.38.

376
00:31:36.593 --> 00:31:38.022
<v Tutor>Not significant.

377
00:31:38.022 --> 00:31:46.060
<v Tutor>There was no difference in any single component, or in seven-day mortality, so there's no safety signal, and no benefit either.

378
00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:47.645
<v Tutor>Limitations.

379
00:31:47.645 --> 00:31:53.034
<v Tutor>It's elective surgery, not shock, so be careful about extending it to our patients.

380
00:31:53.034 --> 00:31:55.522
<v Tutor>The intervention can't be blinded.

381
00:31:55.522 --> 00:32:01.504
<v Tutor>And the composite is driven by events, like kidney injury, that have many causes beyond flow.

382
00:32:01.504 --> 00:32:02.688
<v Tutor>How it fits.

383
00:32:02.688 --> 00:32:05.176
<v Tutor>This is the latest in a long line.

384
00:32:05.176 --> 00:32:19.805
<v Tutor>Shoemaker's supranormal targets in the 1980s gave way to goal-directed therapy, and then to OPTIMISE in 2014, where a cardiac output guided algorithm reduced complications from 43.4 to 36.6 percent, but the confidence interval crossed one.

385
00:32:19.805 --> 00:32:24.721
<v Tutor>A single-centre German pilot of the personalised approach looked promising.

386
00:32:24.721 --> 00:32:28.511
<v Tutor>PELICAN is the large multicentre test, and it's neutral.

387
00:32:28.511 --> 00:32:30.466
<v Tutor>Three take-home messages.

388
00:32:30.466 --> 00:32:38.106
<v Tutor>One: targeting the patient's own baseline cardiac index did not reduce major complications after major abdominal surgery.

389
00:32:38.106 --> 00:32:42.666
<v Tutor>Two: it caused no harm, so it's a neutral result, not a negative one.

390
00:32:42.666 --> 00:32:48.648
<v Tutor>Three: across intraoperative and ICU trials, chasing a flow number in everyone keeps failing.

391
00:32:48.648 --> 00:32:53.090
<v Tutor>What works is treating hypoperfusion, and stopping when it's fixed.

392
00:32:53.090 --> 00:32:55.459
<v Tutor>Does it change your exam answer?

393
00:32:55.459 --> 00:32:56.940
<v Tutor>Only in one line.

394
00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:05.366
<v Tutor>If asked about cardiac output monitoring, you can now say that goal-directed and individualised flow targets have not shown benefit in large trials.

395
00:33:05.366 --> 00:33:10.440
<v Tutor>The summary here is drawn from the published abstract, flagged by Critical Care Reviews.

396
00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:13.268
<v Tutor>Syllabus round-up.

397
00:33:13.268 --> 00:33:18.813
<v Tutor>Five items today, all from week one, acid-base and electrolytes, so they're beyond this week.

398
00:33:18.813 --> 00:33:22.712
<v Tutor>First, blood gas analysis, and how the machine can mislead you.

399
00:33:22.712 --> 00:33:29.027
<v Tutor>This is the analytical side, and it is examined, usually as "what are the sources of error in this result".

400
00:33:29.027 --> 00:33:30.893
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 57 covers it.

401
00:33:30.893 --> 00:33:36.220
<v Tutor>What it is: the analyser measures pH, carbon dioxide and oxygen directly with electrodes.

402
00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:39.789
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate and base excess are calculated, not measured.

403
00:33:39.789 --> 00:33:42.699
<v Tutor>Lactate is measured with an enzyme electrode.

404
00:33:42.699 --> 00:33:44.840
<v Tutor>Four points the examiner wants.

405
00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:47.751
<v Tutor>One, pre-analytical errors are the commonest.

406
00:33:47.751 --> 00:33:53.791
<v Tutor>An air bubble pulls the oxygen towards about 150 millimetres of mercury and lowers the carbon dioxide.

407
00:33:53.791 --> 00:34:03.235
<v Tutor>Delay at room temperature lets leucocytes keep consuming oxygen and producing acid, which matters with a very high white count, where it's called leucocyte larceny.

408
00:34:03.235 --> 00:34:08.561
<v Tutor>Too much liquid heparin dilutes the sample and lowers the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate.

409
00:34:08.561 --> 00:34:10.703
<v Tutor>Two, the patient's temperature.

410
00:34:10.703 --> 00:34:12.954
<v Tutor>Gases are measured at 37 degrees.

411
00:34:12.954 --> 00:34:18.939
<v Tutor>Alpha-stat management uses the uncorrected values, and pH-stat corrects to the patient's temperature.

412
00:34:18.939 --> 00:34:22.728
<v Tutor>Know which one you're quoting after bypass or in hypothermia.

413
00:34:22.728 --> 00:34:24.320
<v Tutor>Why does that matter?

414
00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:30.734
<v Tutor>As blood cools, carbon dioxide becomes more soluble, so its partial pressure falls and the pH rises.

415
00:34:30.734 --> 00:34:40.316
<v Tutor>Cold-blooded animals simply let that happen, and keep the charge state of the imidazole groups on histidine constant, which is where alpha-stat gets its name.

416
00:34:40.316 --> 00:34:43.086
<v Tutor>Their proteins and enzymes keep working.

417
00:34:43.086 --> 00:34:47.992
<v Tutor>Hibernating mammals do the opposite and add carbon dioxide, which is pH-stat.

418
00:34:47.992 --> 00:34:54.053
<v Tutor>In adult cardiac surgery, alpha-stat is usual and is thought to preserve cerebral autoregulation.

419
00:34:54.053 --> 00:34:58.729
<v Tutor>pH-stat raises cerebral blood flow and is used in some paediatric bypass.

420
00:34:58.729 --> 00:35:01.672
<v Tutor>Name both and say which one your unit uses.

421
00:35:01.672 --> 00:35:03.346
<v Tutor>Three, interferences.

422
00:35:03.346 --> 00:35:13.159
<v Tutor>Ethylene glycol's metabolite glycolate cross-reacts with the lactate oxidase on some analysers, giving a falsely high lactate on the gas but a normal lab lactate.

423
00:35:13.159 --> 00:35:14.891
<v Tutor>That is a lactate gap.

424
00:35:14.891 --> 00:35:20.778
<v Tutor>Methaemoglobin and carboxyhaemoglobin make pulse oximetry unreliable, so you need co-oximetry.

425
00:35:20.778 --> 00:35:24.299
<v Tutor>Four, sodium and potassium on the gas versus the lab.

426
00:35:24.299 --> 00:35:29.436
<v Tutor>A direct electrode on whole blood is not affected by high lipids or high protein.

427
00:35:29.436 --> 00:35:33.419
<v Tutor>The indirect lab method is, which causes pseudohyponatraemia.

428
00:35:33.419 --> 00:35:36.940
<v Tutor>It's asked often, usually embedded in data questions.

429
00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:41.935
<v Tutor>Second, interpreting arterial blood gases.

430
00:35:41.935 --> 00:35:48.406
<v Tutor>This is the most examined data skill in the paper, and the College wants a method.

431
00:35:48.406 --> 00:35:51.354
<v Tutor>Write it the same way every time.

432
00:35:51.354 --> 00:35:53.511
<v Tutor>Step one, oxygenation.

433
00:35:53.511 --> 00:35:59.551
<v Tutor>Calculate the alveolar to arterial gradient using the alveolar gas equation.

434
00:35:59.551 --> 00:36:07.029
<v Tutor>Alveolar oxygen equals the inspired fraction times 713, minus the carbon dioxide divided by 0.8.

435
00:36:07.029 --> 00:36:12.925
<v Tutor>The normal gradient rises with age, roughly age divided by four plus four.

436
00:36:12.925 --> 00:36:20.187
<v Tutor>And state the PaO2 to FiO2 ratio for ARDS severity: 300, 200 and 100 are the Berlin cut-offs.

437
00:36:20.187 --> 00:36:28.599
<v Tutor>Here's a worked example of step one, because the alveolar gas equation is where the 2013 question went wrong.

438
00:36:28.599 --> 00:36:31.907
<v Tutor>FiO2 0.6, carbon dioxide 40, PaO2 120.

439
00:36:31.907 --> 00:36:35.646
<v Tutor>Inspired oxygen: 0.6 times 713 is about 428.

440
00:36:35.646 --> 00:36:38.450
<v Tutor>Minus 40 over 0.8, which is 50.

441
00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:40.895
<v Tutor>Alveolar oxygen about 378.

442
00:36:40.895 --> 00:36:44.202
<v Tutor>Gradient, 378 minus 120, which is 258.

443
00:36:44.202 --> 00:36:46.215
<v Tutor>That's a huge shunt.

444
00:36:46.215 --> 00:36:52.255
<v Tutor>Write all three lines of the working, because the College marks the working.

445
00:36:52.255 --> 00:36:57.288
<v Tutor>Step two, the primary disorder from the pH and carbon dioxide.

446
00:36:57.288 --> 00:37:01.027
<v Tutor>Step three, compensation, with the formulas.

447
00:37:01.027 --> 00:37:07.786
<v Tutor>Metabolic acidosis, Winters: one and a half times bicarbonate plus 8, plus or minus 2.

448
00:37:07.786 --> 00:37:12.100
<v Tutor>Metabolic alkalosis: 0.7 times bicarbonate, plus 20.

449
00:37:12.280 --> 00:37:19.265
<v Tutor>Respiratory acidosis: bicarbonate rises 1 per 10 of carbon dioxide acutely, and 4 per 10 chronically.

450
00:37:19.265 --> 00:37:24.135
<v Tutor>Respiratory alkalosis: falls 2 per 10 acutely, 5 per 10 chronically.

451
00:37:24.135 --> 00:37:29.646
<v Tutor>Step four, the anion gap, without potassium, normal 12, corrected for albumin.

452
00:37:29.646 --> 00:37:31.889
<v Tutor>Step five, the delta ratio.

453
00:37:31.889 --> 00:37:34.900
<v Tutor>Below 0.4, a normal anion gap acidosis.

454
00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:38.425
<v Tutor>0.4 to 0.8, mixed high and normal gap acidosis.

455
00:37:38.425 --> 00:37:40.924
<v Tutor>1 to 2, pure high gap acidosis.

456
00:37:40.924 --> 00:37:46.307
<v Tutor>Above 2, a concurrent metabolic alkalosis or a chronic respiratory acidosis.

457
00:37:46.307 --> 00:37:51.561
<v Tutor>Step six, if the gap is high and the cause isn't obvious, the osmolar gap.

458
00:37:51.561 --> 00:37:57.393
<v Tutor>Measured minus calculated, where calculated is twice sodium plus glucose plus urea.

459
00:37:57.393 --> 00:37:59.251
<v Tutor>Above 10 is abnormal.

460
00:37:59.251 --> 00:38:02.455
<v Tutor>The trap the College sets most: pregnancy.

461
00:38:02.455 --> 00:38:09.760
<v Tutor>Normal carbon dioxide in late pregnancy is about 30 millimetres of mercury, so a value of 40 is retention.

462
00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:13.581
<v Tutor>Third, diabetes insipidus.

463
00:38:13.581 --> 00:38:24.758
<v Tutor>You scored five on the 2018 first sitting question 5 on the 15th of October, and part 5.2 was where the marks went, because the diagnosis was missed.

464
00:38:24.758 --> 00:38:27.464
<v Tutor>Let me read it, word for word.

465
00:38:27.464 --> 00:38:36.150
<v Tutor>"A 47-year-old, previously well, 70 kg male was admitted to the Emergency Department with agitation and confusion.

466
00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:47.043
<v Tutor>Following catheterisation, his urine output in the next few hours was 300 to 350 ml per hour." Then the results: sodium 169 millimoles per litre.

467
00:38:47.043 --> 00:38:50.104
<v Tutor>Potassium 4.6 millimoles per litre.

468
00:38:50.104 --> 00:38:53.094
<v Tutor>Chloride 105 millimoles per litre.

469
00:38:53.094 --> 00:38:56.227
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate 26 millimoles per litre.

470
00:38:56.227 --> 00:38:59.146
<v Tutor>Glucose 6.6 millimoles per litre.

471
00:38:59.146 --> 00:39:01.852
<v Tutor>Urea 9.6 millimoles per litre.

472
00:39:01.852 --> 00:39:04.984
<v Tutor>Creatinine 115 micromoles per litre.

473
00:39:04.984 --> 00:39:08.117
<v Tutor>Magnesium 0.88 millimoles per litre.

474
00:39:08.117 --> 00:39:10.609
<v Tutor>Albumin 28 grams per litre.

475
00:39:10.609 --> 00:39:13.101
<v Tutor>Protein 58 grams per litre.

476
00:39:13.101 --> 00:39:15.236
<v Tutor>Plasma osmolality 360.

477
00:39:15.236 --> 00:39:17.871
<v Tutor>Urine specific gravity 1.005.

478
00:39:17.871 --> 00:39:21.146
<v Tutor>"a, What is the most likely diagnosis?

479
00:39:21.146 --> 00:39:32.537
<v Tutor>b, How would you manage the hypernatremia?" Polyuria with a dilute urine and a very high plasma osmolality is diabetes insipidus until proven otherwise.

480
00:39:32.537 --> 00:39:36.880
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 69, polyuria in the ICU, is your source.

481
00:39:37.280 --> 00:39:38.364
<v Tutor>Fifth pause.

482
00:39:38.364 --> 00:39:43.351
<v Tutor>Calculate his free water deficit, and tell me how fast you would correct his sodium.

483
00:39:43.351 --> 00:39:44.760
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

484
00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:53.931
<v Tutor>Total body water is 0.6 times 70, which is 42 litres.

485
00:39:53.931 --> 00:39:57.949
<v Tutor>The deficit is 42 times the sum of 169 over 140, minus 1.

486
00:39:57.949 --> 00:40:00.113
<v Tutor>169 over 140 is about 1.21.

487
00:40:00.113 --> 00:40:02.771
<v Tutor>So 42 times 0.21, about 8.7 litres.

488
00:40:02.771 --> 00:40:15.011
<v Tutor>Correction rate: in hypernatraemia that developed over more than about 48 hours, aim for no more than 10 millimoles per litre per day, about 0.5 an hour, with sodium checked hourly at first.

489
00:40:15.011 --> 00:40:17.978
<v Tutor>Faster correction risks cerebral oedema.

490
00:40:17.978 --> 00:40:22.367
<v Tutor>If it was clearly acute, over hours, faster correction is safe.

491
00:40:22.367 --> 00:40:31.455
<v Tutor>The rest of the College answer: assess fluid and cardiac status first, because if he's shocked, he needs isotonic volume before free water.

492
00:40:31.455 --> 00:40:34.855
<v Tutor>Give water as 5 percent dextrose, or enterally.

493
00:40:34.855 --> 00:40:36.647
<v Tutor>Then treat the cause.

494
00:40:36.647 --> 00:40:45.487
<v Tutor>For central diabetes insipidus, desmopressin, starting at about 1 microgram intravenously, and watch for a sudden fall in urine output.

495
00:40:45.487 --> 00:40:52.535
<v Tutor>For lithium-induced nephrogenic disease, stop the lithium, check a level, and use a thiazide or amiloride.

496
00:40:52.535 --> 00:41:00.324
<v Tutor>The examiners wrote that some candidates' fluid regimes were dangerous, so always state your rate and your monitoring.

497
00:41:00.324 --> 00:41:08.360
<v Tutor>The tests that separate central from nephrogenic: a paired plasma and urine osmolality, then the response to desmopressin.

498
00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:12.780
<v Tutor>A rise in urine osmolality of more than 50 percent suggests central disease.

499
00:41:13.180 --> 00:41:17.851
<v Tutor>A neurocritical care angle, because diabetes insipidus is examined there too.

500
00:41:17.851 --> 00:41:22.632
<v Tutor>After pituitary surgery or severe head injury, look for the triphasic response.

501
00:41:22.632 --> 00:41:26.368
<v Tutor>First, diabetes insipidus over a few days from axonal shock.

502
00:41:26.368 --> 00:41:33.347
<v Tutor>Then a phase of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone release as the dying neurones leak stored hormone, so hyponatraemia.

503
00:41:33.347 --> 00:41:39.337
<v Tutor>Then permanent diabetes insipidus if enough of the supraoptic and paraventricular neurones have gone.

504
00:41:39.337 --> 00:41:45.327
<v Tutor>If you give a long-acting desmopressin in phase one, and phase two arrives, the sodium can fall fast.

505
00:41:45.327 --> 00:41:50.492
<v Tutor>And in the potential organ donor after brain death, diabetes insipidus is very common.

506
00:41:50.492 --> 00:41:59.230
<v Tutor>Vasopressin infusion at about 1 to 2.4 units an hour treats both the polyuria and the vasoplegia, and desmopressin can be added for the polyuria alone.

507
00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:04.780
<v Tutor>A sodium above 155 at retrieval is associated with worse liver graft function, so it matters.

508
00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:10.411
<v Tutor>Fourth, the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone.

509
00:42:10.411 --> 00:42:11.927
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 58.

510
00:42:11.927 --> 00:42:22.410
<v Tutor>What it is: antidiuretic hormone released without an osmotic or volume trigger, so water is retained and sodium falls, with the patient clinically euvolaemic.

511
00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:24.810
<v Tutor>The points the examiner wants.

512
00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:26.452
<v Tutor>One, the criteria.

513
00:42:26.452 --> 00:42:30.367
<v Tutor>Plasma osmolality below 275 milliosmoles per kilogram.

514
00:42:30.367 --> 00:42:32.577
<v Tutor>Urine osmolality above 100.

515
00:42:32.577 --> 00:42:37.314
<v Tutor>Urine sodium above 30 millimoles per litre on a normal salt intake.

516
00:42:37.314 --> 00:42:39.082
<v Tutor>Clinical euvolaemia.

517
00:42:39.082 --> 00:42:45.271
<v Tutor>And normal thyroid and adrenal function, which you must check before making the diagnosis.

518
00:42:45.271 --> 00:42:46.786
<v Tutor>Two, the causes.

519
00:42:46.786 --> 00:42:49.375
<v Tutor>Lung disease including pneumonia.

520
00:42:49.375 --> 00:42:54.933
<v Tutor>Intracranial disease, including subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis and trauma.

521
00:42:54.933 --> 00:43:00.427
<v Tutor>Drugs, such as SSRIs, carbamazepine, opioids, cyclophosphamide and vincristine.

522
00:43:00.427 --> 00:43:03.837
<v Tutor>Malignancy, especially small cell lung cancer.

523
00:43:03.837 --> 00:43:06.237
<v Tutor>Pain and nausea after surgery.

524
00:43:06.237 --> 00:43:12.552
<v Tutor>Three, management, following the 2014 European clinical practice guideline on hyponatraemia.

525
00:43:12.552 --> 00:43:25.940
<v Tutor>For severe symptoms, such as seizures or reduced consciousness, give 150 millilitres of 3 percent saline over 20 minutes, check sodium, and repeat up to twice, aiming for a rise of 5 millimoles per litre.

526
00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:32.572
<v Tutor>Then limit the rise to 10 millimoles per litre in the first 24 hours, and 8 in each 24 hours after that.

527
00:43:32.572 --> 00:43:34.645
<v Tutor>Four, the chronic treatment.

528
00:43:34.645 --> 00:43:36.489
<v Tutor>Fluid restriction first.

529
00:43:36.489 --> 00:43:42.998
<v Tutor>Then urea, or a vasopressin receptor antagonist such as tolvaptan, with care around rapid overcorrection.

530
00:43:42.998 --> 00:43:47.088
<v Tutor>The trap: cerebral salt wasting after subarachnoid haemorrhage.

531
00:43:47.088 --> 00:43:51.408
<v Tutor>It looks the same in the blood, but the patient is volume depleted.

532
00:43:51.408 --> 00:43:55.440
<v Tutor>Fluid restricting that patient risks vasospasm and infarction.

533
00:43:56.340 --> 00:43:58.588
<v Examiner>A quick probe on that trap.

534
00:43:58.588 --> 00:44:01.607
<v Examiner>Day six after subarachnoid haemorrhage.

535
00:44:01.607 --> 00:44:06.681
<v Examiner>Sodium 127 millimoles per litre, urine sodium 80, urine osmolality 520.

536
00:44:06.681 --> 00:44:14.260
<v Examiner>How do you tell cerebral salt wasting from inappropriate antidiuretic hormone, and does it change what you do?

537
00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.464
<v Candidate>The blood and urine look the same, so it comes down to volume.

538
00:44:18.464 --> 00:44:24.760
<v Candidate>Salt wasting has a negative fluid balance, weight loss, a rising urea and haematocrit, and a high urine output.

539
00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:28.993
<v Candidate>Inappropriate hormone release is euvolaemic with a normal or low output.

540
00:44:28.993 --> 00:44:30.792
<v Candidate>It does change management.

541
00:44:30.792 --> 00:44:34.813
<v Candidate>After subarachnoid haemorrhage I wouldn't fluid restrict either way.

542
00:44:34.813 --> 00:44:41.480
<v Candidate>I'd give isotonic or hypertonic saline to keep the patient euvolaemic, and fludrocortisone can reduce the natriuresis.

543
00:44:41.980 --> 00:44:42.787
<v Examiner>Correct.

544
00:44:42.787 --> 00:44:45.712
<v Examiner>In subarachnoid haemorrhage, don't restrict fluid.

545
00:44:45.712 --> 00:44:47.780
<v Examiner>That single line is worth a mark.

546
00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:51.501
<v Tutor>Fifth, electrolyte replacements.

547
00:44:51.501 --> 00:44:57.244
<v Tutor>The syllabus asks you to know the doses, and the College asks them inside bigger questions.

548
00:44:57.244 --> 00:44:58.289
<v Tutor>Potassium.

549
00:44:58.289 --> 00:45:01.596
<v Tutor>Peripheral infusions up to 10 millimoles an hour.

550
00:45:01.596 --> 00:45:07.571
<v Tutor>Through a central line, up to about 20 to 40 millimoles an hour with continuous ECG monitoring.

551
00:45:07.571 --> 00:45:11.168
<v Tutor>Replace magnesium too, or the potassium won't stay up.

552
00:45:11.168 --> 00:45:12.213
<v Tutor>Magnesium.

553
00:45:12.213 --> 00:45:18.246
<v Tutor>10 to 20 millimoles over 20 minutes to an hour in an emergency, such as torsades or arrhythmias.

554
00:45:18.246 --> 00:45:21.669
<v Tutor>Watch reflexes and blood pressure in renal failure.

555
00:45:21.669 --> 00:45:34.723
<v Tutor>In eclampsia, the regimen is different: a loading dose of 4 grams over 15 to 20 minutes, then 1 gram an hour for 24 hours, and the patellar reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output are your monitoring for toxicity.

556
00:45:34.723 --> 00:45:35.767
<v Tutor>Phosphate.

557
00:45:35.767 --> 00:45:44.180
<v Tutor>Severe hypophosphataemia, below 0.32 millimoles per litre, causes respiratory muscle weakness, failure to wean and myocardial depression.

558
00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:49.691
<v Tutor>Replace with about 0.3 to 0.5 millimoles per kilogram intravenously over 6 to 12 hours.

559
00:45:49.691 --> 00:45:52.940
<v Tutor>Monitor calcium, because phosphate will drop it.

560
00:45:53.120 --> 00:46:07.128
<v Tutor>And in refeeding, prevent it rather than chase it: start feeding at about 10 kilocalories per kilogram per day in high-risk patients, give thiamine before the first feed, and check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily for the first few days.

561
00:46:07.128 --> 00:46:12.388
<v Tutor>This was one of the omissions in the hypophosphataemia question on the 14th of October.

562
00:46:12.388 --> 00:46:13.274
<v Tutor>Calcium.

563
00:46:13.274 --> 00:46:18.645
<v Tutor>10 millilitres of 10 percent calcium gluconate gives 2.2 millimoles of elemental calcium.

564
00:46:18.645 --> 00:46:24.846
<v Tutor>The same volume of calcium chloride gives 6.8 millimoles, three times as much, but it burns if it leaks.

565
00:46:24.846 --> 00:46:30.826
<v Tutor>Use it for hyperkalaemia with ECG changes, calcium channel blocker toxicity and massive transfusion.

566
00:46:30.826 --> 00:46:33.041
<v Tutor>And sodium, which we've covered.

567
00:46:33.041 --> 00:46:35.920
<v Tutor>Correction limits matter more than the dose.

568
00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:42.508
<v Tutor>Because electrolyte replacement questions often turn on hyperkalaemia, here are the emergency numbers.

569
00:46:42.508 --> 00:46:53.702
<v Tutor>Stabilise the membrane first with calcium if the ECG has changed: 10 millilitres of 10 percent calcium chloride or 30 millilitres of 10 percent gluconate, repeated if the ECG doesn't improve.

570
00:46:53.702 --> 00:47:08.047
<v Tutor>Shift potassium with 10 units of short-acting insulin and 50 millilitres of 50 percent glucose, which lowers potassium by about 0.6 to 1 millimole per litre within 30 minutes, and check glucose hourly for six hours because hypoglycaemia is common.

571
00:47:08.047 --> 00:47:12.153
<v Tutor>Salbutamol, 10 to 20 milligrams nebulised, adds a similar effect.

572
00:47:12.153 --> 00:47:15.472
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate helps mainly when there is an acidosis.

573
00:47:15.472 --> 00:47:21.885
<v Tutor>Then remove potassium: loop diuretics if the kidneys work, a potassium binder, and dialysis if they don't.

574
00:47:21.885 --> 00:47:26.160
<v Tutor>The calcium chloride versus gluconate point leads to the last pause.

575
00:47:26.560 --> 00:47:28.734
<v Tutor>Sixth and last pause, a quick one.

576
00:47:28.734 --> 00:47:37.014
<v Tutor>Ten millilitres of 10 percent calcium gluconate versus ten millilitres of 10 percent calcium chloride: how many millimoles of elemental calcium in each?

577
00:47:37.014 --> 00:47:38.360
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

578
00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:45.632
<v Tutor>Gluconate, 2.2 millimoles.

579
00:47:45.632 --> 00:47:47.449
<v Tutor>Chloride, 6.8 millimoles.

580
00:47:47.449 --> 00:47:49.320
<v Tutor>About three times as much.

581
00:47:49.320 --> 00:47:54.000
<v Tutor>That's one of the numbers that examiners expect you to know without thinking.

582
00:47:55.400 --> 00:47:57.044
<v Tutor>Before Monday, one thing.

583
00:47:57.044 --> 00:48:04.315
<v Tutor>Take the post-valve gas from today, write it out by hand in the six steps, with every calculation shown, and time yourself to six minutes.

584
00:48:04.315 --> 00:48:07.004
<v Tutor>Then do the same with the gas in today's plan.

585
00:48:07.004 --> 00:48:11.686
<v Tutor>The marks in the acid-base questions came from the calculations you didn't write down.

586
00:48:11.686 --> 00:48:14.574
<v Tutor>That's the fastest win available to you this week.

587
00:48:14.574 --> 00:48:16.417
<v Tutor>This has been Part Two Daily.

588
00:48:16.417 --> 00:48:18.160
<v Tutor>Good luck with today's set.
